{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1474481-5th-edition-disciplines-system","id":1474481,"title":"5th edition disciplines system","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-02-12T00:18:55-08:00","id":1474481,"title":"5th edition disciplines system","content":"One of things I liked most about NWOD was how disciplines worked - attribute + ability + discipline level. This meant your power level increased the odds of success. Is the same system in 5th edition? Would be a plus in terms of encouraging me to get it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1440-lordofash","name":"LordOfAsh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1440_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T01:17:49-08:00","id":1474482,"title":"","content":"Very similar, in V5, you do roll Attribute + Discipline level: Abilities have been disconnected from Disciplines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T01:35:13-08:00","id":1474484,"title":"","content":"That's interesting. I never liked how in the older rules a vampire could have dominate 5 and still be bad at it. Any rationale for removing abilities? Sounds like it removes redundancy and allows vampires to be powerful in disciplines without sinking points into mundane stuff","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1440-lordofash","name":"LordOfAsh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1440_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T01:39:24-08:00","id":1474485,"title":"","content":"Would also allow for more variability in characters with same disciplines as they wouldn't have to sink points into same abilities","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1440-lordofash","name":"LordOfAsh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1440_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T01:54:48-08:00","id":1474487,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I think those were the rationales, freeing PCs from the inevitable Abilities to focus on their character concepts","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-13T19:41:59-08:00","id":1474593,"title":"","content":"I do wish V5 used Requiem\u2019s system. I like that leveling up a discipline increases your dice pool, but Attribute + Discipline means you can\u2019t dip into a discipline for a single dot without being completely mediocre at it. It also devalues skills, which is a shame.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26481-tiltowait","name":"tiltowait","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-13T19:58:01-08:00","id":1474594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tiltowait</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1474481-5th-edition-disciplines-system?p=1474593#post1474593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do wish V5 used Requiem\u2019s system. I like that leveling up a discipline increases your dice pool, but Attribute + Discipline means you can\u2019t dip into a discipline for a single dot without being completely mediocre at it. It also devalues skills, which is a shame.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Depends on the discipline, your rating in the attribute, and your Blood Potency. <br /> <br /> Most disciplines work well, even if just dipping, but especially Celerity, Oblivion, Protean and, to a lesser extent, Dominate, Potence and Fortitude. An attribute of 3 or 4, paired with BP 2 will work well. With higher Blood potency, having a lot of low rated disciplines becomes really more viable, and powerful, if you reach the level you can re-roll rouse checks.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27145-setitefriend","name":"SetiteFriend","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"255"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T05:23:30-08:00","id":1474612,"title":"","content":"For me, the VTR2e way makes better internal sense, because it is logical that in order to be an uber manipulator with Dominate, you'd have high ratings in, say, Persuasion. But this model wouldn't translate perfectly to V5 because Requiem has a target number of 8+ and V5 has a target number of 6+.<br /> <br /> So, that means you can expect about 1/3 of your dice pool as successes in VTR2e (10s always count twice, so the probability on each die is 0.33), whereas you'd expect 1/2 in V5 (10s only count twice if matched, so the chance is about 0.5 per die, give or take some faff). Therefore, it makes sense that Requiem uses three traits for its pools while Masquerade only uses two. (VTR2e also only requires one success to achieve your goal, whereas V5 requires 3+, usually.)<br /> <br /> But, I think the way V20 and earlier handled it (with an Attribute + Ability) meant characters were more consistent in their mundane and supernatural abilities. A Gangrel or Nos probably took both Animalism and Animal Ken, for example, which meant they didn't just sink their XP into one and they therefore had mundane ways to deal with things.<br /> <br /> For example, when I was making a Hecata character for V5, I realised that Occult was completely irrelevant for my character to use Necromancy; they only needed dots in Oblivion to use those powers. That might incentivise players to ignore Skills and focus on Disciplines instead, since those are usually more powerful and a lot of the utility powers are free (or get a re-roll at BP1).<br /> <br /> That said, XP is much rarer in V5, so having fewer things to spend it on makes sense. And you could argue that the previous method of calculating Discipline dice pools incurred an XP tax (via required Abilities). So, YMMV, I guess?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T06:41:45-08:00","id":1474621,"title":"","content":"It makes sense to me that you can be really experienced with something and still be bad at it. There are also a lot of things that you can learn and find you've got a lot of talent in them. <i>Command </i>and <i>Forgetful Mind</i> are both Dominate abilities but it seems rather intuitive that someone who's imposing would have an easier time with the former while someone who's good at reassuring would do better with the latter.<br /> <br /> <br /> I like single discipline dips. IMO there's a paradigm where the first dot of a discipline should be cheap and broadly applicable and useful for night-to-night hunting while later powers should be seldom-used special abilities.<br /> <br /> For me, as restricting as it might be to need to put dots into the right attributes and abilities, a Dominate master without good social skills or a necromancer without occult is just stupid. (also, like, V20 was more forgiving; you could totaly get by with 3 or 4 dots in something in V20, while you need six dice to reliably succeed in &quot;normal&quot; tasks in V5, so there's more stress to optimize.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 02-14-2022, 09:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T08:38:10-08:00","id":1474626,"title":"","content":"I'd say the Requiem arrangement of Attribute + Ability + Discipline makes for a good balance overall, where both brute supernatural might and a natural talent coming from being already experienced in a sort of mindset or kind of action can count towards one's proficiency with certain powers, not to mention giving a PC or NPC pretty respectable dice pools even without too much optimization.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T10:13:45-08:00","id":1474634,"title":"","content":"I prefer it without the ability checks. Somebody having the power to control what people do or read minds is a bit less impressive when they're already rather capable of talking people into doing what they want or understanding a person's thoughts by mundane means.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T14:41:00-08:00","id":1474649,"title":"","content":"At first I was puzzled by the end of Ability checks but I think it's brilliant. <br /> <br /> It's a bit like OD&amp;D with Thief abilities: everyone can try to hide but only the Thief can Hide in the Shadows. <br /> <br /> Disciplines do not make you better at doing mundane things: they impose themselves over reality, unchecked Powers of the Blood. <br /> <br /> It's very thematic and also opens up character concepts: no longer the inevitable Abilities selected for high rolls limiting your background choices.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T22:32:28-08:00","id":1474705,"title":"","content":"On the otherhand, It often encourages you to build characters around what their disciplines don't do.<br /> The best ventrue is a social tertiary; he doesn't need social skills. He has Dominate and Presence, which make up for his lack of social skills. A wise ventrue is thus a social idiot savant who puts all his points into mentals and physicals. <br /> (some disciplines still encourage related skills; potence won't activate if you can't hit anyone, which in turn is a little crazy because a simple strength increase should be something that lets you get away with low combat stats)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T23:32:22-08:00","id":1474706,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1474481-5th-edition-disciplines-system?p=1474705#post1474705\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the otherhand, It often encourages you to build characters around what their disciplines don't do.<br /> The best ventrue is a social tertiary; he doesn't need social skills. He has Dominate and Presence, which make up for his lack of social skills. A wise ventrue is thus a social idiot savant who puts all his points into mentals and physicals.<br /> (some disciplines still encourage related skills; potence won't activate if you can't hit anyone, which in turn is a little crazy because a simple strength increase should be something that lets you get away with low combat stats)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It can be a side effect in the direction of min-maxing, though you still need Attributes working with Discipline ratings. You can probably out-win skilled mortals with relevant powers, either by buffs (Potence) or through insta-passing powers (Dominate), especially when taking into account also Blood Surge, but then you'll be at disadvantage with your peer vampires who are more in synch with the relevant area of expertise of their Disciplines. Which is good, I feel, it reinforces the theme of the game: you will be exhilarated and intoxicated by your powers when moving around your preys, but then ... there are bigger fish in the lake.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12590,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","user":"Manfr","date":". 02-15-2022, 02:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-15T01:41:03-08:00","id":1474712,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1474481-5th-edition-disciplines-system?p=1474705#post1474705\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the otherhand, It often encourages you to build characters around what their disciplines don't do.<br /> The best ventrue is a social tertiary; he doesn't need social skills. He has Dominate and Presence, which make up for his lack of social skills. A wise ventrue is thus a social idiot savant who puts all his points into mentals and physicals. <br /> (some disciplines still encourage related skills; potence won't activate if you can't hit anyone, which in turn is a little crazy because a simple strength increase should be something that lets you get away with low combat stats)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this. As you mentioned upthread, it also makes you more likely to min-max because of the requirement to optimise to succeed at basic things (3 successes = 6 dice, usually). So you have Hecata bruisers who start with 3-4 dots in Oblivion but no Occult, and never have to take a dip into that Skill. They just make sure they have the right Attributes to power their Disciplines.<br /> <br /> Though, it's trivially easy to change how V5 works. You can just add an Ability of the ST's choosing instead of the Discipline rating.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-15T04:25:50-08:00","id":1474720,"title":"","content":"In VtM &amp; WtA the supernatural power can be seen as an extension-extrapolation of one's talents, skills &amp; knowledge, while the Awakened, Changelings and Wraiths wield their power in a more raw, direct manner, from their will/desire/supernal experience than any mundane experience or study. At least at first glance - odd things like Vicissitude and Body Alteration/Crafts do put my generalist assertion to question.<br /> <br /> <br /> In the end it's a matter of game aesthetics, style and familiarity/nostalgia at play in what arrangements one is more comfortable with at the table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 02-15-2022, 07:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-15T18:56:38-08:00","id":1474771,"title":"","content":"Even if you start a campaign with older characters that can buy several dots in their clan disciplines, at some point in the characters' history they didn't have those and would have had to get by on their Abilities and the most basic levels of supernatural powers. You might not mechanically need to buy dots of Occult or Leadership to use Oblivion or Dominate, but someone proficient in those disciplines really ought to be at least somewhat capable in the analogous skills.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}